# Trained models.
#
# These ARE tracked, deliberately. They are what the server loads at startup,
# and committing them means a clone runs immediately instead of needing a
# training pass first -- which matters when the person cloning is marking the
# project rather than developing it.
#
# The whole set is ~5 MB, fine for ordinary git objects. Do NOT put them in
# Git LFS: LFS has already destroyed data in this repository twice, leaving
# three-line pointer files where the content should be.
#
# They must stay in step with the scikit-learn pin in requirements.txt (1.9.0),
# because a model saved by one major version does not reliably load in another.
# `python train_server_models.py` rebuilds all of them, and render.yaml runs it
# during deployment so a hosted instance always trains against its own
# installed version rather than trusting these files.
models/*
!models/categorizer.joblib
!models/anomaly.joblib
!models/anomaly_category_stats.json
!models/forecaster.joblib
!models/forecaster_columns.json
!models/daily_forecaster.joblib
!models/provenance.json

# Real user corrections -- training data, not source. Keep local.
data/corrections.csv

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
